<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Bws.Core\Bws.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    WPF UI, MIT, the base of this window's appearance since 2026-08-02. ADR-24 and ADR-15
    carry the decision, docs/10-WPF-UI.md carries what it costs and the five traps that are
    not in their documentation.

    Version pinned rather than floating. A control library that changes what the product looks
    like because a restore picked up a newer build is a change nobody decided.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="WPF-UI" Version="4.3.0" />

    <!--
      CsWin32, for one call: telling the window manager that the title bar above this window is a
      dark one. Merging the library's dark dictionaries styles everything INSIDE the window and
      says nothing about the bar, so Windows drew its own in #4C4A48 against content at #202020 -
      measured on screen 2026-08-10, backlog 148.
      A build time generator rather than a new dependency for the product, which is the same
      standing this package already has in Bws.Core - `ADR-2` and `ADR-15` say so in as many
      words, and PrivateAssets keeps it out of what ships.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.CsWin32" Version="0.3.298">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Per-monitor DPI awareness, ADR-25. WPF is system DPI aware by default, which means Windows
    stretches the window bitmap on any monitor whose scaling differs from the one the session
    logged on with - and stretched text is blurry in the literal sense. Measured before this
    line existed: the process reported SYSTEM_AWARE.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <!-- CsWin32 emits a pointer signature for DwmSetWindowAttribute, backlog 148. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      WithCulture=false is load-bearing here for the same reason it is in Bws.Cli: MSBuild
      reads the ".en." in the name as a culture and compiles the file into a satellite
      assembly instead of embedding it, stripping the culture out of the resource name on the
      way. The build stays green and the window comes up showing keys instead of words.

      These are plain data we load and choose between ourselves, not .NET satellite
      resources. Every gui.<language>.json added later needs the same two attributes.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Resources\gui.en.json"
                      LogicalName="Bws.Gui.Resources.gui.en.json"
                      WithCulture="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Windows shows this as the file description, in properties and in Task Manager. -->
    <AssemblyTitle>Better Windows Services</AssemblyTitle>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>